{"reference":"Job 33","book":"Job","chapter":33,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading Elihu within Job","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Job 33 begins Elihu’s response after Job and the three friends have stopped speaking. Elihu says he had held back because of his youth, yet he is angry with both Job and the friends; he then delivers four uninterrupted speeches before the divine answer. Readers differ over his role: some see him as preparing the way for that answer, while others see him as another inadequate defender of retribution. The narrative’s later silence about Elihu does not settle the question.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_033_326ad1c13381ef3b90c80702"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"Elsewhere, Job is associated with endurance amid suffering and with a compassionate outcome from the Lord. That description need not portray endurance as silent acceptance of abuse or as a barrier to seeking protection and escape; it can describe persistence through the larger story and its outcome.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_033_1fb76d10ab6459740dc49a8e"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}